## 1. Introduction
Among various alternatives of secondary energy, hydrogen energy is regarded as an ideal clean energy source because of its large calorific value and environmentally friendly combustion products [1]. For recommending large-scale production of hydrogen, water electrolysis strategy has attracted numerous attentions. The electrocatalytic water splitting is comprised of two basic reactions, known as the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [2–4]. Until now, Pt-based materials for HER and noble-metal oxides $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ ) for OER are regarded as the most outstanding electrocatalysts, respectively. Nevertheless, the limited resource, high cost and needy long-term stability have impeded their widespread practical application [5,6]. Thereby, tremendous works have been devoted to exploit nonnoble metal electrocatalyst with high efficiency and superior stability [7–10].
The cubic pyrite-phase $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ , as regular earth-plentiful catalysts, have attracted much attention for both HER and OER due to the well-matched electronic configuration of Co metal-ion [11–13]. The 3d bands of Co ion could further split into $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ and $\mathbf{e_{g}}$ sub-bands by crystalline field. The ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-t}}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ band is completely filled with six electrons while the ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-e}}_{\mathrm{g}}$ band has one electron per Co atom [13]. A near-unity occupancy of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e_{g}}}$ orbital of transition metal ions favors the intrinsic OER activity in alkaline solution [14]. $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ exhibits $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}^{6}\mathbf{e}_{\mathbf{g}}^{1}$ electronic configuration near the optimal $\mathbf{e_{g}}$ filling which meets the demand of an ideal OER catalysts. In addition, the 3d orbital of Se may participate in bonding with metal atoms due to its energy level being close to the 3s and $3{\tt p}$ orbitals. Such electron structure leads to more metallicity which endows the fast migration between the interface of electrode and electrolyte [15,16]. Cobalt pyrite $(\mathsf{C o S}_{2})$ displays suitable chemisorption energy of atomic hydrogen on catalyst surface, as well as intrinsically high conductivity, which ensures its advantages for HER process [17,18].
The electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ could be further improved through heteroatom doping, defect engineering, as well as hetero-junction establishment because of their positive effect on tuning electronic structure, optimizing electrical conductivity, and enriching the active sites [19]. Transitional metal ions (Ni, Fe and Mn) and hetero-atom (S, P, N, Se) have been substituted at the cation and anion sites of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}/\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ , respectively, thanks to their analogous ionic radius and similar crystal structure [20–23]. Yu et al. revealed P-substituted $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ electrocatalysts could introduce more vacancies around Co cations [20]. Wei et al. reported a plasma nitridation method for preparation of ${\sf N-C o S e}_{2}@{\sf C P}$ electrodes which could provide plentiful active sites and lower the over-potential for HER and OER in alkaline medium [21]. The substitution of S elements into $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ nanosheets could lower the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption [22]. Anion substitution induced vacancy chemistry has been suggested as a valid policy for modulating the electronic configuration and changing the adsorption/desorption energy of the reaction intermediates. Thereby, extensive works have been performed for vacancy research in transition metal dichalcogenides. However, the clear relationship between vacancy concentration and electrocatalytic water splitting performance has not been established yet.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis methods
To fabrication of ZIF-67 nanosheet, the ZIF-67 polyhedral were firstly synthesized based on previous works [24]. Afterward, the ZIF-67 nanoparticles were dispersed into methanol to form a suspension. The suspension was further reacted with $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ solution in the Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclaves at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After washing in methanol for several times and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , the final yellow product was denoted as Co-MOF-NS [25]. For synthesis of $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\times)}\mathrm{Se}_{2\times}@\mathrm{NC}$ nanomeshes, the Co-MOF-NS powder $\langle100\mathrm{mg}\rangle$ was placed at the downstream side of a tube furnace, and a mixture of sulfur and selenium powder was placed at the upstream side of the tube furnace. The mixture was heated at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ under argon (Ar) atmosphere. The mass of sulfur powder was controlled to be $200~\mathrm{mg}$ unchanged, and different amounts of selenium powder were added to get several products with different sulfur and selenium mole ratios, which were denoted as $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.75}\mathrm{Se}_{0.25}@\mathrm{NC}$ , $\cos_{1.5}5\mathrm{e}_{0.5}@\mathsf{N C}$ $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ ${\mathsf{C o S S e@N C}}$ , $\mathrm{CoS}_{0.75}\mathrm{Se}_{1.25}@\mathrm{NC}$ , and $\mathrm{CoS}_{0.5}\mathrm{Se}_{1.5}@\mathrm{NC}$ , respectively. For comparison, ${\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ and ${\mathrm{CoSe}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ were also prepared. The details for materials synthesis, characterization technique, electrochemical measurements and DFT calculations were shown in the Supporting Information.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterizations
Scheme 1 displays the synthesis process of cobalt sulfoselenide nanomeshes $(\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC})$ . In brief, the rhombic dodecahedron ZIF-67 nanoparticles converse to ultrathin MOF nanosheets after solvothermal treatment. Subsequently, a series of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2(1-\mathbf{x})^{-}}$
$S e_{_{2\mathrm{x}}}@\mathsf{N C}$ nanomeshes were fabricated by calcining S and Se power with ultrathin metal–organic framework nanosheets.
SEM images in Fig. 1(a-f) present the structure and morphology of the as-prepared $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples. The ZIF-67 rhombic dodecahedron converse to hollow structure composed of ultrathin cobalt-based MOF nanosheets after solvothermal reaction in the presence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ (Fig. 1a, b). The $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples maintain the morphology of precursors and large amounts of pores appear after high-temperature calcination (Fig. 1c–f). Notably, the morphology of $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ collapses when the temperature reaches $600~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ (Fig. S1). Fig. S2 exhibits the SEM images of $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples as $\times$ varying from 0 to 1. The ultrathin nanosheets gradually change to interconnected nanoparticles as the increasing of Se content. The high-resolution TEM images are displayed in Fig. $1(\mathrm{g-i})$ . The adjacent lattice fringe with a spacing of $0.233\ \mathrm{nm}$ could be contributed to the (211) orientation in $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ . The interplanar spacing is smaller than that of ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ $(0.239\mathrm{~\nm})$ but larger than that of ${\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ $(0.226\ \mathrm{nm})$ [26,27]. The elemental distribution mapping proves the Co, S, Se, C and N elements are homo-distributed in $\cos_{1.25^{-}}$ $\mathsf{S e}_{0.75}@\mathsf{N C}$ (Fig. 1j). Fig. 2 displays the high-resolution STEM images of $\mathsf{C o S1.25S e0.75@N C}$ . The introduction of Se heteroatoms breaks the symmetry crystal lattices of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ and induces the formation of vacancies/related disorder area in the crystal structure. It agrees well with previous works which also prove anion-doping is an effective approach to induce vacancy in transition metal chalcogens [28–30].
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of all $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples are exhibited in Fig. 3a. The peaks at around $32.4^{\circ}$ , $36.3^{\circ}$ , $39.8^{\circ}$ , 46.4, $55.1^{\circ}$ , $57.6^{\circ}$ , $60.2^{\circ}$ and $62.7^{\circ}$ correspond to the (200), (2 1 0), (2 1 1), (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (2 2 2), (23 0) and (3 21) crystal planes of poly-crystal pyrite structure $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ (JCPDS Card No. 65–3322), respectively [31]. The diffraction peaks at $30.7^{\circ}$ , $34.6^{\circ}$ , $35.8^{\circ}$ , $40.2^{\circ}$ , $47.6^{\circ}$ and $50.1^{\circ}$ could be well indexed to the (101), (111), (120), (210), (211) and (002) lattice planes of the orthorhombic-phased $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}[32]$ . The lower angle shift of the major diffraction peaks as the increasing of Se content could be ascribed to the gradual shrinkage of the unit cells, which further proves the blending of Se and S at the atomic level (Fig. S3a). The XRD patterns agree well with the HRTEM results (Fig. 2). The peak intensity of $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\times)}\mathrm{Se}_{2\times}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples decreases gradually because the alloying reaction disorders the crystal structure [29]. Specially, the crystal vacancy and distortion could supply more reaction active sites for electrocatalysis. As seen in Fig. 3b, the actual Se-to-S atomic ratio $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{S e t o}\mathsf{S}}\right)$ in $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ materials are in good agreement with the starting precursors.
The Raman spectroscopy further analyze the structural composition of $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}.$ . As displayed in Fig. 3c, two peaks around $1570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the graphitic G band and the disorder-induced D band, respectively [33]. The characteristic broad peaks appear in all $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\times)}\mathrm{Se}_{2\times}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples, proving the organic ligands converse to carbon matrix. The introduction of a suitable carbon matrix is an effective strategy to enhance electrical conductivity and structural stability of electrocatalysts. The intensity ratio of D band (disordered structure of carbon material) to G band (graphitized structure) reflects the degree of structural graphitization of carbon matrix. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratio of the $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples is 0.89, which is higher than that of ${\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ (0.68) and ${\mathrm{CoSe}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ (0.71). The carbon disorder and vacancies are introduced by hetero- $.N.$ -element doping, which could induce more active sites for electrocatalytic reaction. For ${\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ the characteristic peaks at 325, 401, and $410~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the $\mathtt{E}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ , $\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{g}}$ and $\mathrm{T_{g}}$ modes of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ phase. For $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}@\mathsf{N C}$ , the peaks at around 457, 504 and $656~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ further prove the cubic phase of $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ [34,35].
Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) analysis were performed to prove the vacancy concentration of all $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples [28]. As displayed in Fig. 3d, a signal at the $\mathbf{g}.$ -factor $\phantom{0}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}=2.003,$ can be attributed to the charge state of anion vacancies in $\mathrm{CoS}_{_{2(1-x)}}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ . The anion vacancy concentration increases as the $S e/\bar{S}$ ratio raising from 0 to 0.75, and it decreases after further increasing the $S\mathrm{e}/S$ ratio. The concentration of the anion vacancy is in the following order: $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}>\mathrm{CoSSe@NC}>\mathrm{CoS}_{1.5}\mathrm{Se}_{0.5}@-$ ${\mathrm{NC}}>{\mathrm{CoS}}_{1.75}{\mathrm{Se}}_{0.25}@{\mathrm{NC}}>{\mathrm{CoSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}>{\mathrm{CoS}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ . Interestingly, EPR analysis prove the vacancy concentration could be optimized by changing the $S\mathrm{e}/S$ ratio [36]. The charge redistribution neighboring the cobalt atoms occur owing to the existence of vacancies, thus leading to charge enrichment of the metal atoms near the vacancies. Fig. 3(e and f) show the calculation value of vacancy formation energy in $\mathrm{CoS}_{2(1-\mathrm{x})}\mathrm{Se}_{2\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{NC}$ samples. A small fractions of Se substitution considerably reduce the vacancy formation energy through retention of a high density of Co-coordinated terminal anions. The calculated optimal Se ratio is consistent with ESR results.
