vity. The OER stability of the $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ catalyst is further assessed by CV scanning in $0_{2}$ -saturated $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ (inset in Fig. 5g). In addition, the current density shows no apparent change after $55\mathrm{h}$ test, demonstrating superior OER stability could be obtained on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{CoS}}_{1.25^{-}}$ $\mathsf{S e}_{0.75}@\mathsf{N C}$ catalyst (Fig. 5g). The $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ exhibits excellent OER activity as compared to recently reported works (Fig. 5h) [42,44–46,51–53].
Furthermore, XPS was carried out on $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ catalyst to investigate the chemical states and surface composition after OER process. The peak centered at $529.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the O 1s region demonstrates the –OOH generation (Fig. 5e). In addition, from Co 2p spectrum, the ratio of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ becomes higher after OER treatment, indicating the transformation of $C0^{2+}$ to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ during OER process (Fig. 5f) [54]. The XPS analysis results prove the generation of cobalt oxyhydroxide (Co-OOH) on the surface of $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ catalyst.
According to previous publications, Co-OOH is considered as the actual active intermediate for water oxidation process [51–54].
The above electrochemical reaction activity demonstrates that the $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ catalyst may be a promising bifunctional candidate for overall water splitting reaction (Fig. 6a). Therefore, the overall water splitting reaction was performed by using $\cos_{1.25^{-}}$ $\mathsf{S e}_{0.75}@\mathsf{N C}$ catalyst as both the cathode and anode in 1 M KOH using the two electrodes configuration (Fig. 6b). As presented in Fig. 6c, the $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ requires an overpotential of $440~\mathrm{mV}$ at a cell voltage of $1.67\mathrm{V}$ to achieve a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The overall water splitting activity of $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ is better than pure ${\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ and ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ and is comparable to recently reported materials (Fig. 6e) [56–61]. ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}@{\mathsf{N C}}$ shows better activity for water splitting at more positive potentials due to its fast increase trend. After $60\mathrm{~h~}$ long-life stability test, no apparent decline could be observed for $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.25}\mathrm{Se}_{0.75}@\mathrm{NC}$ (Fig. 6d), demonstrating its outstanding stability for overall water splitting.
