## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the introduction of Se heteroatom induces the formation of vacancies/related disorder area in the crystal structure. The CoS1.25Se0.75@NC electrocatalyst possesses the largest vacancy concentration as well as the optimal electrocatalytic performance. The vacancies in electrocatalysts alter the local electronic structure, thus leading to unsaturated coordination state and high electrochemical activity. The unique three-dimensional hierarchical architecture assembled by porous nanomeshes could promote the contact between catalyst and electrolyte. Furthermore, the encapsulation of cobalt sulfoselenide nanoparticles into $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon matrix could avoid aggregations, thus leading to superior stability of $\mathrm{CoS1.25Se0.75@NC}$ electrocatalyst. The present work demonstrates a practical approach to modulate the vacancy concentration of cobalt sulfoselenide and provides new ideas for design of efficient non-metal electrocatalysts. However, the interaction between adsorption intermediates and vacancy remains unclear, which requires advanced techniques to study the defect restructuring during electrocatalytic process in the future.
